{
  "points": [
    {
      "description": "Core built-in authentication handlers are provided by Requests itself. The snapshot identifies HTTP Basic authentication through HTTPBasicAuth and Digest authentication through HTTPDigestAuth.BUILD_DIGEST_HEADER, including challenge processing and hashing support for MD5, SHA-1, SHA-256, and SHA-512. HTTPDigestAuth.HANDLE_401 may rewind the request body, construct a Digest Authorization header, resend the request, and preserve the original response in history. The evidence describes functionality and dependencies, but does not provide a security assessment or production-use evidence [Data: Reports (11, 4)].",
      "score": 96
    },
    {
      "description": "Request preparation applies authentication before hooks and transmission. PREPARE_AUTH obtains credentials from the request URL when explicit authentication is absent; a two-item credential tuple is converted into an HTTPBasicAuth object, applied to the request, and followed by content-length recalculation. The related utility requests.utils.get_auth_from_url extracts URL credentials, including percent-encoded credentials and special characters. The owning classes and some external implementations are not fully visible, so the snapshot does not establish all runtime behavior [Data: Reports (11, 18)].",
      "score": 92
    },
    {
      "description": "Sessions hold persistent authentication state alongside other request defaults. REQUESTS.SESSION exposes an auth attribute and supports session-level headers, cookies, proxies, TLS settings, mounted adapters, redirect limits, and environment merging; session examples and tests update authentication and send prepared requests. Environment-related support also references NETRC and environment variables, but the supplied evidence does not show the complete netrc authentication implementation or identify which credentials would be selected at runtime [Data: Reports (5, 12, 7)].",
      "score": 86
    },
    {
      "description": "Redirect processing governs when credentials may be retained or removed. Tests cover retaining credentials for same-host redirects, stripping them on HTTPS-to-HTTP downgrade, and evaluating host and port changes, including equivalent default HTTP and HTTPS ports. requests.utils.urldefragauth removes embedded credentials and URL fragments, while redirect resolution combines URLs and requotes them. These are documented or tested behaviors, not proof of production security outcomes [Data: Reports (12, 18)].",
      "score": 90
    },
    {
      "description": "Proxy authentication is handled at the transport boundary rather than solely by request authentication. HTTPAdapter selects proxy managers, distinguishes SOCKS proxies from other proxies, and uses proxy_headers for non-SOCKS proxies; tests cover Basic proxy authorization headers with populated and empty passwords. SOCKS support is supplied conditionally through urllib3.contrib.socks, so the proxy mechanism includes a third-party urllib3 extension point, while the adapter and proxy-header behavior belong to Requests. The evidence does not show unauthorized use or a deployed proxy configuration [Data: Reports (5, 18, 7)].",
      "score": 88
    },
    {
      "description": "Documentation and tests provide much of the visible authentication evidence. Requests.GET documentation covers authentication parameters, and the test communities exercise Basic authentication, Digest authentication, redirect credential handling, proxy authorization, session authentication, URL credential parsing, and malformed or boundary inputs. HTTPBIN is an external test fixture used to exercise authentication and redirect scenarios; its implementation and ownership are not established. References to requests-threads, grequests, requests-futures, and httpx are examples of asynchronous integrations rather than confirmed authentication components or dependencies of this snapshot [Data: Reports (12, 5, 18, 22)].",
      "score": 82
    }
  ]
}
